<section xml:id="section-homomorphism">
  <title>Homomorphisms</title>
  <p>
    Most of the previous university math courses you may have taken
    have been about functions. The calculus courses are analyses of
    the behaviour of functions. In linear algebra, understanding
    linear transformations is one of the main goals of the course and
    matrices are so vital precisely because they perfectly encode
    linear transformation. Differential equations are explicity
    questions that ask: what function satisfies this rule? Mathematics
    is about functions.
  </p>
  <p>
    This remains very true in abstract algebra. So far we have defined
    the structure of a group and tried to understand that structure.
    It turns out that one of the most important ways to understand
    that structure is to understand the functions that cooperate with
    that structure.
  </p>
  <p>
    What do I mean by <sq>cooperate</sq>? Think back to linear algebra
    again. A linear transformation was a function between vectors
    spaces that satisfied two rules. Let <m>u</m> and <m>v</m> be
    vectors, let <m>a</m> be a scalar, and let <m>M</m> be a linear
    transformation (represented by a matrix, if you wish). These were
    the rules.  <md>
      <mrow>
        M(u+v) = M(u) + M(v)
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        M(au) = aM(u)
      </mrow>
    </md>
    <sq>Cooperate</sq> means that the two operations that define a
    vector space<mdash/>vector addition and scalar
    multiplication<mdash/>can be performed either before or after the
    function. The function <emph>preserves</emph> those operations. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Something similar can be said about limits and continuity. In
    calculus, we work with continuous functions. The definition of
    continuity may not have been stated this way in the calculus you
    studied, but one valid way to define continuity is as follow. Let
    <m>f</m> be a function and let <m>a</m> be a point in its domain.
    Then the function is continuous at <m>a</m> if the following is
    true. 
    <me>
      \lim_{x \rightarrow a} f(x) = f \left( \lim_{x \rightarrow a} x
      \right)
    </me>
    A function is continuous if it cooperates with limits, if it
    preserves limits. The important operation here, the limit, can be
    done either before or after the function.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same idea holds for groups, leading to the main definition of
    this section.
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>G</m> and <m>H</m> be groups (written multiplicatively
        as usual) and let <m>phi: G \rightarrow H</m> be a function
        beteween them. The <m>\phi</m> is a <term>homomorphism</term>
        if, for all <m>g_1, g_2 \in G</m>, the following equation
        holds.
        <me>
          \phi(g_1g_2) = \phi(g_1) \phi(g_2)
        </me>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    The definition relates the two binary operations of the two
    groups. On the left, the binary operation is happening in
    <m>G</m>, before the function acts. On the right, the binary
    operation is happening in <m>H</m>, after the function acts. 
  </p>
  <p>
    As usual, let me define some examples. 
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      I start this section talking about linear transformations. They
      are homomorphisms. <m>\RR^n</m> and <m>\RR^m</m> are groups
      under addition. A matrix, acting as a linear transformation,
      satisfies this equation.
      <me>
        M(u+v) = M(u) + M(v)
      </me>
      This is precisely the definition of a homomorphism between these
      two group.s
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      The function <m>\phi: \ZZ \rightarrow \ZZ</m> defined by
      <m>\phi(n) = -n</m> is a homomorphism (with the understanding
      that two negative signs cancel and that <m>-0 = 0</m>). Let me
      verify the condition. Let <m>n</m> and <m>m</m> be integers.
      <me>
        \phi(n+m) = -(n+m) = (-n) + (-m) = \phi(n) + \phi(m) 
      </me>
      Here, the fact that a negative sign (or, equivalently,
      multiplying by <m>-1</m>) distributes proves that this is a
      homomorphism. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      The function <m>\phi: \QQ^* \rightarrow \QQ^*</m> defined by
      <m>\phi(r) = r^2</m> is a homomorphism. Again, let me verify the
      required identity. LEt <m>r, s, \in \QQ^*</m>.
      <me>
        \phi (rs) = (rs)^2 = r^2 s^2 = \phi(r) \phi(s) 
      </me>
      Here, the rules of exponents prove that this is a homomorphism. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      The function <m>e^x: \RR \rightarrow (0, \infty)</m> is a group
      homomorphism. This one is more interesting: the domain is a
      group under addition and the codomain is a group under
      multiplciation. Let me verify the required identity.
      <me>
        e^{(x+y)} = e^x e^y
      </me>
      There's not much to actually do here: this equation is a law of
      exponent. That law of exponents gives me exactly what I need:
      addition before and multiplication after. 
    </p>
    <p>
      The exponential is an important example of a group homomorphism
      between different kinds of structures, addative and
      multiplicative. The idea of an exponential like this shows up
      all over mathematics, not just for real-valued functinos. 
    </p>
    <p>
      If the exponential changes from addition to multiplciation, then
      the logarithm show go the other way. Indeed, the logarithm is a
      group homomorphism <m>\ln: (0, \infty) \rightarrow \RR</m> from
      a multiplicative group to an addative group. Again, I verify the
      required equation.
      <me>
        \ln(ab) = \ln a + \ln b 
      </me>
      Again, there isn't much to do here: the equation I need is
      already just a law of logarithms. It's multiplication before and
      addition afterwards. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      The determinant <m>det: GL(n) \rightarrow \RR^*</m> is a
      homomorphism from the group fo invertible matrices to the group
      of non-zero real number under multiplication. Why is this true?
      Well, here is one of the rules of determinants, where <m>M</m>
      and <m>N</m> are matrices. 
      <me>
        \det(MN) = \det(M)\det(N)
      </me>
      This rule is precisely what is needed for the definition. On the
      left, I have matrix multiplication. On the right I have the
      ordinary real number multiplication of the two determinants. 
    </p>
    <p>
      This is a good example of a homomorphism between very different
      groups. Matrix and numbers are dissimilar mathematical objects,
      but there is still a relationship between them given by this
      homomophism. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      Let <m>a \in RR</m>. Then the evaluation function at <m>a</m>,
      <m>f_a: \RR[x] \rightarrow \RR</m> is a function which take a
      polynomial <m>p(x)</m> and evaluates it to produce <m>p(a)</m>,
      which is a real number. This is a homomorphism from the group of
      polynomials under addition to the group of real number under
      addition. Let me verify the required equation, using <m>p</m>
      and <m>q</m> as two polynomials. 
      <me>
        f_a(p + q) = (p + q)(a) = p(a) + q(a) = f_a(p) + f_a(q)
      </me>
      What does this mean? Well, if I add two polynomials and
      evaluate, that the same as evaluting and adding them. This is
      certainly true for polynomials, making the evaluation function a
      homomorphissm. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Like the previous, this is a good example of a homomorphism
      between different kinds of mathematical objects: this time,
      numbers and polynmoials. Similar evaluation homomorphism can be
      defined for groups of functions as well. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      Let <m>n \in ZZ</m>. Then the map which is reduction modulo n is
      a homomorphism <m>\pi_n: \ZZ \rightarrow \ZZ/n\ZZ</m>. This map
      sense every integer to its class modulo n. The equation I want
      is essentially the following statement, where the bar indicated
      reduction modulo n. 
      <me>
        \overline{a+b} = \overline{a} + \overline{b}
      </me>
      This is true of the integers, basically by the simple fact that
      arithmetic works modulo n. 
    </p>
    <p>
      This is a good example of a homomorphism from an infinite group
      to a finite group. As in the previous examples, homomorphism can
      relate different kinds of groups, and it is good to notice that
      they can go between groups of very different cardinalities as
      well. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      Let me define a function <m>\phi: \ZZ/10\ZZ^* \rightarrow
      \ZZ/4\ZZ</m>. I define this explicitly, term by term.
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \phi(1) = 0
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \phi(3) = 1
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \phi(7) = 3
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \phi(9) = 2
        </mrow>
      </md>
      This is a homomorphism. How do I prove that? I don't have a nice
      rool to use here, but these are small groups. I use just check
      all the cases explicitly, perhaps using a predetermined Caley
      table for the multiplication. For example, I can explicitly
      check that <m>\phi(3\cdot 9) = \phi(7) = 3 = 1 + 2 = \phi(3) +
      \phi(9)</m>, so the homomorphism equation works for these two
      elements. In a group of four elements, there are only twelve
      multiplications to check (6 pairs and, in general, checking both
      orders of multiplications) For small groups, we can sometimes
      work explicitly like this.
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      The notation <m>C^1(\RR)</m> is the set of continuously
      differentiable functions with domain <m>\RR</m>. The derivative
      <m>\frac{d}{dx}: C^1(\RR) \rightarrow C(\RR)</m> is a
      homomorphism to the set of continuous function on <m>\RR</m>.
      Why? Well, I have the linearity of the derivative.
      <me>
        \frac{d}{dx} (f + g) = \frac{df}{dx} + \frac{dg}{dx} 
      </me>
      This derivative rule is exactly the rule needed to show that
      this is a homomorphism between two groups that use addition as
      the binary operation. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Similar homomorphisms can be made with other differential
      operations, as well as with indefinite integrals, since they are
      also linear. 
    </p>
    <p>
      I can also use definite integrals. The notation <m>C([0,1])</m>
      is the group (under addition) of continuous functions wtih
      domain <m>[0,1]</m>. I can define a function using an indefinite
      integral.
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \phi: C([0,1]) \rightarrow \RR
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \phi(f) = \int_0^1 f(x) dx 
        </mrow>
      </md>
      Then I have the linearity rule for the integral. 
      <me>
        \int f(x) + g(x) dx = \int f(x) dx + \int g(x) dx 
      </me>
      Again, this rule precisely shows that <m>\phi</m> is a
      homomorphism. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      I'll finish with some example that apply to many groups. As I've
      said before, mathematicians really enjoy the special cases of
      definitions at the margin. These are important, even if they
      seem obvious or silly. 
      <ul>
        <li>
          The identity function <m>\Id: G \rightarrow G</m> on any
          group is a homomorphism. 
        </li>
        <li>
          Let <m>e_h</m> be the identity of a group <m>H</m>. Then,
          for any group, the map <m>\phi: G \rightarrow H</m> defined
          by <m>\forall g \in G, \phi(g) = h_e</m> is a homomorphism.
          It just sends everything to the identity, which makes
          checking the condition no work at all. This is often called
          the trivial homomorphism between two groups.
        </li>
        <li>
          Let <m>H \subset G</m> be a subgroup of a group. The
          inclusion function <m>i: H \rightarrow G</m> is the map
          defined by <m>i(h) = h</m>. It just sends all elements of
          the subgroup to themselves as elements of the larger group.
          (This inclusion function exists for any subset in set throgy
          -- it's not just defined for subgroups.) The inclusion of a
          subgroup is always a homomorophism. Again, this is almost
          nothing to check, but this is an important definition. 
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </example>
  <p>
    Now on to properties of homomorphisms.
  </p>
  <proposition xml:id="proposition-identity-to-identity">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\phi: G \rightarrow H</m> be a homomorphism between two
        groups. The <m>\phi</m> sends the identity of the first group
        to the identity of the second. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        Let <m>e_g</m> be the identity of <m>G</m>, let <m>e_h</m> be
        the identity of <m>H</m> and let <m>g \in
        G</m> be any element. Then consider this calculation.
        <me>
          e_h \phi(e_g) = \phi(e_g) = \phi(e_g e_g) = \phi(e_g)
          \phi(e_g) 
        </me>
        The first equality is the definition of the inverse in
        <m>H</m>, the second equality is the definition of the inverse
        in <m>G</m>, and the third is the group homomorphism property.
        Then I can apply cancellation on the right. 
        <me>
          e_h = \phi(e_g)
        </me>
        This completes the proof. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <proposition xml:id="proposition-homomorphism-inverse">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\phi: G \rightarrow H</m> be a homomorphism of groups.
        Then for any <m>g \in G</m>, <m>\phi(g^{-1}) = \left( \phi(g)
        \right)^1</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        Consider this calculation.
        <me>
          \phi(g^{-1}) \phi (g) = \phi(gg^{-1}) = \phi(e_g) = e_h
        </me>
        I can repeat this calculation in the opposite order.
        <me>
          \phi(g) \phi(g^{-1}) = \phi(g^{-1}g) = \phi(e_g) = e_h
        </me>
        This is the proof that <m>\phi(g^{-1})</m> is the inverse of
        <m>\phi(g)</m> in the group <m>H</m>, which proves the
        proposition. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <p>
    There are two very important subgroups associated to be
    homomorphism. 
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p> 
        Let <m>\phi: G \rightarrow H</m> be a homomorphism of groups.
        The <term>kernel</term> of <m>\phi</m> is the subset of all elements
        of <m>G</m> that are sent to the identity in <m>H</m>. The
        <term>image</term> of <m>\phi</m> is the subset of all
        elements of <m>H</m> which are outputs of the function.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <proposition xml:id="proposition-image-kernel">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\phi: G \rightarrow H</m> be a homomorphism of groups.
        Then the kernel is a subgroup of <m>G</m> and the image is a
        subgroup of <m>H</m>. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        I'll start with the kernel. I know from <xref
        ref="proposition-identity-to-identity"/> that the identity is
        sent to the identity, so <m>e_g</m> is in the kernel. Then if
        <m>g_1, g_2</m> are both in the kernel, I can calculate
        <me>
          \phi(g_1 g_2) = \phi(g_1) \phi(g_2) = e_h e_h =e_h 
        </me> 
        showing that the product is also in the kernel.
        Finally, if <m>g</m> is in the kernel, then using <xref
        ref="proposition-homomorphism-inverse"/>, I can calculate
        <me>
          \phi(g^{-1}) = \left( \phi(g) \right)^{-1} = e_h^{-1} = e_h 
        </me>
        showing that the inverse is also in the kernel. This proves
        that the kernel is a group. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Now for the image. The identity is always in the image
        according to <xref ref="proposition-identity-to-identity"/>.
        If <m>h_1, h_2</m> are in the image, then there exist <m>g_1,
        g_2</m> such that <m>\phi(g_1) = h_2</m> and <m>\phi(g_2) =
        h_2</m>. Then I can calculate
        <me>
          h_1h_2 = \phi(g_1) \phi(g_2) = \phi(g_1 g_2)
        </me>
        showing that the product is in the image. Finally, using <xref
        ref="proposition-homomorphism-inverse"/> I can also
        calculate
        <me>
          h_1^{-1} = \left( \phi(g_1) \right)^1 = \phi(g_1^{-1})
        </me>
        showing that inverses are also in the image. I conclude that
        the image is a subgroup. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <p>
    Kernels are, in particular, useful for properties of
    homomorphisms. 
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\phi: G \rightarrow H</m> be a homomorphism of groups.
        Then <m>\phi</m> is injective if and only if <m>\Ker(\phi)</m>
        is <m>\{e_g\}</m>. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        This is an if and only if proof. The forward implication is
        very short: if the function is injective and the identity goes
        to the identity by <xref
        ref="proposition-identity-to-identity"/>, then nothing else
        can be sent to the identity, so the kernel is <m>\{e_g\}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the reverse implication, assume the kernel is
        <m>\{e_g\}</m>. Then, in the standard setup for an injectivity
        proof, assume that <m>\phi(g_1) = \phi(g_2)</m>. I can do this
        calculation, using the homomorphism property and <xref
        ref="proposition-homomorphism-inverse"/>. 
        <me>
          \phi(g_1g_2^{-1}) = \phi(g_1) \left(\phi(g_2) \right)^{-1} =
          \phi(g_2) \left( \phi(g_2) \right)^{-1} = e_h
        </me>
        Since the kernel is only <m>e_g</m>, this mean that 
        <me>
          g_1 g_2^{-1} = e_g 
        </me>
        and by multiplying on the right by <m>g_2</m>, I conclude
        <me>
          g_1 = g_2
        </me>
        This shows that the function is injective, completing the
        if-and-only-if proof. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <p>
    Let me get down some more basic properties of homomorphisms
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\phi: G \rightarrow H</m> and <m>\psi: H \rightarrow
        K</m> be homomorphisms of groups. Then <m>\psi \circ \phi: G
        \rightarrow k</m> is also a homomorphism of groups. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        This is just a calculation using the homomorphism properties
        of the two pieces of the composition.
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \psi \circ \phi (g_1g_2) = \psi(\phi(g_1g_2)) =
            \psi(\phi(g_1) \phi(g_2)) = \psi\phi(g_1)) \psi\phi(g_2)
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            = (\psi \circ \phi (g_1)) (\psi \circ \phi (g_2)) 
          </mrow>
        </md>
        This is precisely the desired homomorphism property of the
        composition. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\phi:G \rightarrow H</m> be a homomorphism of groups.
        If <m>K \subset G</m> is a subgroup of <m>G</m>, then
        <m>\phi(K)</m> is a subgroup of <m>H</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        <m>K</m> is a group and <m>\phi</m> acting on the group
        preserves the binary operation. Therefore, <m>\phi \big|_{K}:
        K \rightarrow H</m> is also a homomorphism of groups. This
        means that, according to <xref
        ref="proposition-image-kernel"/>, the image, <m>\phi(K)</m> is
        a subgroup of <m>H</m>. This completes the proof. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
</section>
